const content = {
  "references/examples.md": "# Examples — repurposing end to end\n\nTwo worked flows: one atom fanned out to five native versions, and a long-form piece atomized.\nVoice is the blunt indie-founder voice from earlier skills (short, dry, no exclamation marks,\nconcrete). Your output uses the loaded `voice.md`.\n\n---\n\n## Example 1 — one atom → five native versions\n\n**The atom:** we deleted our most-requested feature because nobody used it; the \"boring\"\nreplacement became the most-used in two days. Lesson: build less, watch behavior not requests.\n\n### Instagram — Reel\n- **Hook (visual):** hand hitting \"delete\" on a block of code. **On-screen:** \"Deleting 6 months\n  of work.\" **Spoken:** \"We just deleted our most-requested feature.\"\n- Beats: the why → the boring fix → the result. **CTA:** save this for your next roadmap meeting.\n\n### LinkedIn — text post\n- **Hook line:** \"'Requested' is the most expensive word in product.\"\n- Whitespace-formatted, expands the lesson (built for words, not behavior), ends on a genuine\n  question. **CTA:** \"Anyone actually build straight from requests and have it work?\" Link in\n  comments if any.\n\n### X — single post\n- \"'Customers requested it' is how you justify six months building something nobody uses.\n  Deleted ours today. Watching behavior from now on.\"\n- Two lines, the point up front. No hashtags.\n\n### Instagram — carousel\n- Cover: \"We deleted our most-requested feature →\". Slides: 1) the ask vs the use, 2) requested ≠\n  used, 3) build the boring thing, 4) shipping less is a feature. **CTA:** save.\n\n### Pinterest — pin\n- Keyword-rich: \"Product roadmap lesson: why customer requests mislead | build less\". Image: the\n  \"requested vs used\" contrast graphic. Links to the full blog. Evergreen, searchable.\n\n**Notice:** same atom, five genuinely different builds — different hooks, formats, lengths, CTAs,\nregister. None is a copy-paste of another.\n\n**The bad version (avoid):** the LinkedIn paragraph pasted identically into IG, X, and a Pinterest\ndescription. Fits none, looks like spam to anyone following on two, and underperforms everywhere.\n\n---\n\n## Example 2 — long-form → many atoms\n\n**Source:** a 1,800-word blog post, \"10 things we learned in our first year.\"\n\n**Atomize (don't summarize):** pull the strongest standalone atoms —\n1. the deleted-feature lesson (→ the five posts above),\n2. \"our first hire was a mistake — here's the signal we missed,\"\n3. \"the pricing change that doubled revenue,\"\n4. \"the channel we wasted 6 months on,\"\n5. \"the one metric we should've watched sooner.\"\n\nEach atom → its own native post(s) across platforms. One blog → ~3 weeks of content. Optionally,\none post points to the full blog (the hero link), but every atom stands alone.\n\n**Anti-pattern:** \"Just published a new blog: 10 things we learned 👇 [link]\" as the *only* post.\nThat throws away nine atoms.\n\n---\n\n## What both examples show\n\n- **Atomize first**, then build native — don't translate a wrapper.\n- Each version is **different and stands alone**.\n- **Voice constant, register shifts** per platform.\n- Long-form yields **many** posts, not one summary.\n- Quality per platform is the gate; volume is the byproduct.\n",
  "references/platform-transforms.md": "# Platform Transforms — one atom, native everywhere\n\nHow the *same* core idea becomes each platform's native post. These are transform recipes: feed\nin the atom, get out a version built for that platform. Each changes the hook, format, length,\nmechanics, and register — never just the hashtags.\n\n> For full caption mechanics see `caption-writer/references/platforms.md`; for hook craft per\n> format see `hook-writer/references/formats.md`. This reference is the *transform* layer that\n> sits on top of both. Re-verify platform specs periodically.\n\n---\n\n## → Instagram\n\n- **Native forms:** Reel (video), carousel (visual teaching), single image.\n- **Pick by atom:** a *moment/story* → Reel; a *how-to/list* → carousel; a *single point* →\n  image.\n- **Hook:** visual + on-screen text; caption first line within ~125 chars.\n- **Mechanics:** link in bio; a few relevant hashtags. Register: warm.\n- **CTA:** save / send / follow.\n\n## → TikTok\n\n- **Native form:** short video, 9:16, sound-on, trend/sound-aware.\n- **Transform:** make it faster and looser than the Reel; lead with the hook in frame one;\n  keyword the caption for search.\n- **Register:** casual, native, less polished. (Hand to `tiktok-script` for trend-led ideas.)\n- **CTA:** comment / follow.\n\n## → LinkedIn\n\n- **Native form:** text post (or a document/PDF carousel).\n- **Transform:** expand the atom into a short, whitespace-heavy post; lead with the hook line\n  (~140-char cutoff); more authority/insight register.\n- **Mechanics:** link in comments (not body); 3–5 hashtags. Longer is fine if it earns the read.\n- **CTA:** a genuine question (comments) or a soft point.\n\n## → X / Twitter\n\n- **Native form:** a single punchy post, or a thread for multi-atom ideas.\n- **Transform:** strip to the sharpest line; lead with the point; cut everything else.\n- **Mechanics:** 0–2 hashtags; conversational. (Hand multi-atom ideas to `thread-writer`.)\n- **CTA:** reply / repost, or none — a strong last line earns engagement.\n\n## → Threads\n\n- **Native form:** short, conversational post or light thread.\n- **Transform:** reactive, opinion-forward, lower-polish than LinkedIn; one topic tag.\n- **CTA:** invite replies.\n\n## → Bluesky\n\n- **Native form:** short text post; links are clickable and welcome (no link penalty culture).\n- **Transform:** like X but warmer/less combative; skeet-length punchy take; hashtags barely used.\n- **CTA:** reply / repost.\n\n## → Facebook\n\n- **Native form:** text + image/video post; group-friendly, conversational.\n- **Transform:** slightly longer and more personal than X; lead with a relatable line; minimal\n  hashtags; links fine in the post.\n- **CTA:** comment / share.\n\n## → YouTube Shorts\n\n- **Native form:** short vertical video.\n- **Transform:** like the Reel, but the first line doubles as a **searchable** title; the\n  lean-in audience tolerates a slightly stronger payoff.\n- **CTA:** subscribe / watch the long version.\n\n## → Pinterest\n\n- **Native form:** a pin — image (or video) + **destination link** + keyword-rich description.\n- **Transform:** make it evergreen and **searchable** (keywords, not hashtags); the visual must\n  stand alone; the link is the point (real traffic driver).\n- **CTA:** save / visit the link.\n\n---\n\n## The transform table (quick reference)\n\n| Platform | Native form | Length | Hook | Links | Register |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Instagram | Reel / carousel / image | short–med | visual + text | bio | warm |\n| TikTok | short video | short | frame-one | bio | casual |\n| LinkedIn | text post / doc | med–long | line + whitespace | comments | authority |\n| X | post / thread | short | the point | inline | conversational |\n| Threads | post / light thread | short | the take | inline | casual |\n| Bluesky | short post | short | the take | inline | casual-warm |\n| Facebook | text + media post | short–med | relatable line | inline | personal |\n| YT Shorts | short video | short | searchable line | description | clear |\n| Pinterest | pin + link | short | keyword | clickable | descriptive |\n\nSame atom, nine different builds. If your versions don't differ across these columns, you\nhaven't repurposed — you've relocated.\n",
  "references/repurposing-model.md": "# The Repurposing Model — Atomize → Adapt\n\nThe method that separates real repurposing from lazy reposting. Internalize this and every\noutput is native; skip it and you produce duplicate spam.\n\n## Why copy-paste fails\n\nPasting the same caption everywhere fails three ways at once:\n\n- **It fits none of them.** A LinkedIn-length post dies on X; a TikTok caption looks lazy on\n  LinkedIn. Each platform has its own format, mechanics, and audience mindset.\n- **It looks like spam.** People who follow you on two platforms see the identical post twice and\n  unfollow one.\n- **It gets suppressed.** Several platforms down-rank obviously cross-posted content (e.g.,\n  recycled Reels with another app's watermark, duplicate text).\n\nNative-everywhere beats same-everywhere, every time.\n\n## The model\n\n**Atomize → Adapt.**\n\n### Atomize — extract the reusable core\nThe **atom** is the smallest reusable unit of value in the source: the point, the insight, the\nstory, the stat, the moment, the quote. It's platform-agnostic — it's *what you're saying*, not\n*how*.\n\n- A single post usually contains **one atom**.\n- A long piece (blog, 20-min video, podcast, newsletter) contains **many atoms** — each strong\n  enough to be its own post. The skill of long-form repurposing is *finding the atoms*, not\n  summarizing the whole.\n\n### Adapt — rebuild the atom natively per target\nTake the atom and build it fresh for each platform: a new hook, the right format and length,\nnative mechanics, the right CTA, the right register. You're not translating the wrapper — you're\nrebuilding it around the same core.\n\n## What stays vs what changes\n\n| Stays constant | Changes per platform |\n|---|---|\n| The **atom** (core idea) | The **hook** (per platform's mechanics) |\n| The **brand voice** | The **format** (Reel / carousel / thread / post) |\n| The **truth** / facts | The **length** and structure |\n| The brand **guardrails** | The **CTA** (matched to platform + goal) |\n| | **Mechanics** — hashtags, links, tags |\n| | **Register** (voice tone-map: formal ↔ loose) |\n\nIf your two versions differ only in hashtags, you changed nothing that matters — go back and\nre-hook, re-format, re-length.\n\n## The directions of repurposing\n\nEach direction has different mechanics:\n\n- **One → many platforms (fan-out):** one atom, re-natived for each platform. The most common.\n- **Long → short (atomize):** a blog/video/podcast → many short posts, one per atom. The biggest\n  multiplier — one \"hero\" asset can feed weeks of posts.\n- **One format → many formats:** a written insight → a carousel (slides), a Reel (script), a\n  thread. Same platform or across.\n- **Cross-medium:** video → text (pull the spoken insights as written atoms) and text → video\n  (hand the atom to `reels-script`). Never just transcribe or read aloud.\n- **Across time (recycle):** evergreen atoms re-posted later, refreshed — see `content-recycling`.\n\n## The hero → atoms mental model\n\nThe efficient operation runs on a few **hero assets** (a deep blog post, a long video, a big\nlaunch) that each get **atomized into many small posts** across platforms and time. Plan the\nheroes; the atoms fill the calendar. That's how `batch-content-plan`'s capacity math works\nwithout burning the user out on net-new ideas.\n\n## The gate\n\nVolume is the goal, but **quality per platform is the gate.** Five native posts beat fifteen\ncopy-pastes. If a version doesn't feel built for its platform, it's not done.\n",
  "references/source-types.md": "# Source Types — repurposing by direction\n\nDifferent sources atomize differently. This is how to handle each, so you pull the most posts\nwithout padding.\n\n---\n\n## Long-form → many short posts (the biggest multiplier)\n\nSources: a blog post, a 20-minute video, a podcast episode, a newsletter, a webinar, a talk.\n\n**Method — atomize, don't summarize:**\n1. Read/scan the whole piece and **list every atom** — each distinct insight, story, stat,\n   quote, contrarian take, or step. A solid blog post yields 5–10; a podcast can yield more.\n2. Score the atoms — which are strongest, most surprising, most standalone.\n3. Turn each strong atom into its **own** native post (per `platform-transforms.md`). One atom =\n   one post, not \"here's a summary of my blog.\"\n4. Optionally keep one post that points to the full piece (the \"hero\" link), but the atoms each\n   stand alone.\n\n**Anti-pattern:** compressing a rich 2,000-word post into one thin \"I wrote a blog, check it\nout\" post. That wastes 90% of the material.\n\n## One strong post → fan out (one → many platforms)\n\nSources: a post that did well, or a fresh single idea.\n\n**Method:** it's one atom — re-native it for each target platform (new hook, format, length,\nCTA, register). Space the posts out; vary the angle where audiences overlap. This is the default\nfan-out covered in the main flow.\n\n## One format → many formats\n\nSources: a written insight you want in more shapes.\n\n**Method:** the same atom becomes:\n- a **carousel** (break the point into slides),\n- a **Reel/Short** (hand to `reels-script`),\n- a **thread** (hand to `thread-writer`),\n- a **single post**.\nPick the formats that suit the atom and the platforms; don't force all four.\n\n## Cross-medium (video ↔ text)\n\n- **Video → text:** pull the **spoken insights, quotes, and moments** as written atoms — then\n  build native text posts. Don't dump a transcript; transcripts aren't posts.\n- **Text → video:** hand the atom to `reels-script` to build it *as a video* (hook + beats +\n  shots). Don't read the caption aloud over a static image.\n- **Long video → clips:** see `captions-and-clipping` for cutting clips; pair each clip with a\n  native caption.\n\n## Across time (recycle)\n\nEvergreen atoms can run again later, refreshed (new hook, updated example, different format) so\nit doesn't read as a literal repost. This is the time dimension — see `content-recycling` for\ncadence and refresh rules.\n\n---\n\n## Choosing how far to push it\n\n- A **hero asset** (deep blog, big video, launch) → atomize hard; it can feed weeks.\n- A **single light idea** → one or two native versions; don't manufacture filler to hit a count.\n- The test: every post must clear the quality bar on its own. If you're padding to reach a\n  number, stop — fewer, stronger posts win.\n",
  "SKILL.md": "---\nname: cross-platform-repurposing\ndescription: >-\n  Use to turn one piece of content into multiple platform-native posts — adapt, never\n  copy-paste. Run when the user says \"repurpose this,\" \"turn this into posts for every platform,\"\n  \"cross-post,\" \"make this work on TikTok/LinkedIn/X too,\" \"one idea into a week of content,\"\n  \"turn this blog/video/podcast into social posts,\" or wants to fan content out. Reads\n  brand-profile and voice first, extracts the core idea, then re-natives it for each platform\n  (different hook, format, length, mechanics, CTA) using the content skills, and hands the\n  fan-out to scheduling-and-queue. This is the multiplier that makes a content operation\n  sustainable — but every version must stand on its own and feel native, never duplicated.\nmetadata:\n  version: 1.0.0\nlicense: MIT\n---\n\n# Cross-Platform Repurposing\n\nThis skill turns one idea into many posts — the single biggest lever for producing content\nsustainably. One blog post becomes a week of social; one strong insight becomes five\nplatform-native posts. It's how a small team posts like a big one.\n\nThe principle that makes it work — and the trap that ruins most attempts:\n\n> **Repurposing is not reposting.** Pasting the same caption onto every platform is the lazy\n> version: it fits none of them natively, it looks like spam to anyone who follows you in two\n> places, and several platforms quietly suppress obviously cross-posted content. The craft is\n> **atomize → adapt**: extract the core idea, then rebuild it natively for each target — a\n> different hook, format, length, and CTA, same truth and voice.\n\n## When to use this\n\n- Fan one post out across platforms (one → many).\n- Atomize a long piece (blog, video, podcast, newsletter) into many short posts (long → short).\n- Convert one format into others (a written insight → a carousel, a Reel, a thread).\n- Inside `batch-content-plan`, to stretch limited raw material into a full calendar.\n\n**When NOT to use it:** when content is genuinely platform-specific (an in-joke, a\nplatform-native trend, a reply) — forcing it elsewhere reads wrong. And when the audience\noverlaps almost entirely on two platforms, vary the *angle*, don't just relocate the post. Know\nwhen a piece shouldn't travel.\n\n## Step 0 — Read the foundation first\n\nLoad `brand-profile.md` and `voice.md`. Voice stays constant across platforms; **register\nshifts** per platform (use the voice tone-map — LinkedIn more formal, TikTok looser). Carry\nguardrails everywhere.\n\n## Step 1 — Atomize: find the core idea\n\nExtract the reusable **atom** from the source — the point, the insight, the story, the stat, the\nmoment. For a single post that's usually one atom; for long-form (a blog, a 20-minute video) it's\n**several** atoms, each strong enough to be its own post. Don't summarize a long piece into one\nweak post — pull its best 5–10 atoms. See `references/repurposing-model.md`.\n\n## Step 2 — Pick targets and direction\n\nDecide the **platforms** and the **direction** of the repurpose (one→many, long→short,\nformat→format, video↔text — see `references/source-types.md`). Direction determines the\nmechanics.\n\n## Step 3 — Re-native per platform (don't translate, rebuild)\n\nFor each target, rebuild the atom natively — composing the right skills:\n\n- **A new hook per platform** (`hook-writer`) — what hooks on a LinkedIn feed differs from a\n  TikTok first frame.\n- **The right format and length** for that platform.\n- **The copy** (`caption-writer`), the **script** if video (`reels-script`), or the **thread**\n  (`thread-writer`).\n- **Native mechanics** — hashtags, links (bio vs clickable vs comments), CTA.\n\nWhat stays the same: the **atom** and the **voice**. What changes: hook, format, length,\nstructure, CTA, mechanics, register. See `references/platform-transforms.md`.\n\n## Step 4 — Quality-check: does each stand alone and feel native?\n\nEvery version must pass two tests:\n\n1. **Stands alone** — someone who only sees this one post gets full value (no \"as I said on\n   LinkedIn…\").\n2. **Feels native** — it looks built *for* this platform, not relocated. If you can tell it was\n   copy-pasted, rebuild it.\n\nIf two platforms share most of the audience, change the *angle* between them, not just the\nformatting.\n\n## Step 5 — Hand off to scheduling\n\nPass the finished, per-platform versions to `scheduling-and-queue` to schedule/fan out (it\nconfirms and validates first; WoopSocial applies per-platform fields). Space them out — don't\nfire the same idea everywhere at the same minute.\n\n## Quality bar — self-check\n\n- Did you **atomize** (extract the core idea) rather than translate the wrapper?\n- Is each version **genuinely native** — different hook, format, length, CTA — not copy-paste?\n- Does each **stand on its own**?\n- Is the **voice constant** with the **register shifted** per platform?\n- For long-form, did you pull **multiple atoms**, not one summary?\n- Did you recognize anything that **shouldn't** be repurposed?\n\nIf two versions are the same text with different hashtags, you reposted — go back to Step 3.\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- **Heavy audience overlap across platforms** → vary the angle/format between them so followers\n  don't see the same post twice.\n- **Platform-specific content** (trends, replies, in-jokes) → don't force it elsewhere; say so.\n- **Long-form with one idea** → it's one atom; don't manufacture filler to hit a post count.\n- **Video → text** → pull the spoken insights/quotes as written atoms; don't just transcribe.\n- **Text → video** → hand the atom to `reels-script` to build it as a video, don't read the\n  caption aloud.\n- **Regulated/sensitive** → carry compliance guardrails into every version.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `brand-profile`, `voice-builder` — voice + register + guardrails.\n- `hook-writer` — a fresh hook for each platform.\n- `caption-writer`, `reels-script`, `thread-writer` — build each native version.\n- `content-recycling` — repurposing across **time** (refresh evergreen later).\n- `captions-and-clipping`, `opus-clip` — cutting clips from long video (podcasts, webinars) at depth.\n- `batch-content-plan` — uses this to stretch material into a calendar.\n- `scheduling-and-queue` — schedules/fans out the finished versions.\n\n## References\n\n- `references/repurposing-model.md` — atomize → adapt; what stays vs changes; the directions.\n- `references/platform-transforms.md` — how one idea becomes each platform's native post.\n- `references/source-types.md` — repurposing by source/direction (long-form, fan-out, cross-medium).\n- `references/examples.md` — one idea → five native versions, and a long-form atomization.\n"
};
export default content;
